        "faq": [
          {
            "question": "How do I work out the price of an event ticket?",
            "answerHtml": "<p>Add up all fixed costs plus a 10 % buffer. Decide how many tickets you will realistically sell, for example 75 % of capacity, and divide the fixed costs by that number. Then add VAT, the ticketing fee and other per-ticket costs on top. The result is your minimum price, which you then compare with the local market.</p>",
            "answerText": "Add up all fixed costs plus a 10 % buffer. Decide how many tickets you will realistically sell, for example 75 % of capacity, and divide the fixed costs by that number. Then add VAT, the ticketing fee and other per-ticket costs on top. The result is your minimum price, which you then compare with the local market."
          },
          {
            "question": "What is break-even for an event?",
            "answerHtml": "<p>Break-even is the number of tickets at which income covers all costs. You calculate it as fixed costs divided by the contribution per ticket, which is the price minus VAT, fees and other per-ticket costs. Every ticket above it is profit, every ticket below it a loss.</p>",
            "answerText": "Break-even is the number of tickets at which income covers all costs. You calculate it as fixed costs divided by the contribution per ticket, which is the price minus VAT, fees and other per-ticket costs. Every ticket above it is profit, every ticket below it a loss."
          },
          {
            "question": "How much more should door tickets cost?",
            "answerHtml": "<p>There is no fixed rule. Many organisers add a few euros to the standard advance price so that buying early pays off, without turning walk-ups away. Announce the door price when sales open, and never make the door cheaper than advance tickets.</p>",
            "answerText": "There is no fixed rule. Many organisers add a few euros to the standard advance price so that buying early pays off, without turning walk-ups away. Announce the door price when sales open, and never make the door cheaper than advance tickets."
          },
          {
            "question": "Should I pass the ticketing fee on to buyers?",
            "answerHtml": "<p>It lowers your break-even, in our example from 255 to 238 tickets, but buyers see an uneven total such as €23.35. Many organisers absorb the fee and round the price up instead. Both are legal as long as the advertised price already includes every mandatory fee.</p>",
            "answerText": "It lowers your break-even, in our example from 255 to 238 tickets, but buyers see an uneven total such as €23.35. Many organisers absorb the fee and round the price up instead. Both are legal as long as the advertised price already includes every mandatory fee."
          },
          {
            "question": "Does VAT really make a difference to ticket pricing?",
            "answerHtml": "<p>Yes. On a €22 ticket the VAT ranges from €1.15 in France (5.5 %) to €2.62 in Ireland (13.5 %), and a German club night at 19 % carries €3.51. The rate depends on the country where the event takes place and on whether it counts as a concert or show.</p>",
            "answerText": "Yes. On a €22 ticket the VAT ranges from €1.15 in France (5.5 %) to €2.62 in Ireland (13.5 %), and a German club night at 19 % carries €3.51. The rate depends on the country where the event takes place and on whether it counts as a concert or show."
          }
        ],
